#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# count_if

#% text
# `count_if(xs, p)` gives how many elements `x` of the list `xs` have `p(x)` true.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `p` is a function returning `true` or `false`; an element for which it gives anything else is not counted.

#% item
# `count_if(xs, p)` is `length(select(xs, p))`; it is defined in the prelude.

#% item
# To count elements matching a pattern instead of a test, use `count`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
count_if([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], is_even)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count_if(range(100), k => mod(k, 7) == 0)
#% output
#= 14

#% cell
count_if([], is_odd)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any test function, including ones on strings:

#% cell
count_if(["apple", "fig", "banana", "kiwi"], s => string_length(s) > 4)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
count_if(range(100), is_prime)
#% output
#= 25

#% text
# Elements of any kind:

#% cell
count_if([1, x, 2.5, "a", [1]], is_number)
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The acceptance count of a Markov chain: positions where the state changed.

#% cell
chain = [1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 1]
count_if(range(length(chain) - 1), i => chain[i + 1] !== chain[i])
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# The share of random points inside the unit quarter circle, close to pi/4:

#% cell
seed(5); pts = random_real(1, [2000, 2])
n(count_if(pts, p => p[1]^2 + p[2]^2 < 1)/2000)
#% output
#= 0.7825

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same count from `select`:

#% cell
length(select(range(20), is_odd))
#% output
#= 10

#% cell
count_if(range(20), is_odd)
#% output
#= 10

#% text
# `count` takes a pattern rather than a function:

#% cell
count([1, x, 2, y], _: Int)
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A test that does not decide (here a comparison with a symbol) is not counted:

#% cell
count_if([1, a, 3], x => x > 0)
#% output
#= 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `select`, `count`, `every`, `some`, `tally`
